---
import Adr from "../components/Adr.astro";
import Diagram from "../components/Diagram.astro";
import Excerpt from "../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
import Path from "../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import Sequence from "../components/Sequence.astro";

const PERM = "server/src/iki_server/org/permissions.py";
const ACCESS = "server/src/iki_server/entities/access.py";
const OAUTH = "server/src/iki_server/oauth";

const boxes = [
  { id: "mcp", x: 20, y: 30, w: 190, h: 60, label: "MCP endpoint", sub: "tools call the REST API" },
  { id: "oauth", x: 250, y: 30, w: 190, h: 60, label: "OAuth server", sub: "consent, delegation" },
  { id: "idp", x: 480, y: 30, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Identity provider", sub: "OIDC or SAML, SCIM" },
  { id: "token", x: 250, y: 140, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Token limits", sub: "scopes, projects, alongside" },
  { id: "session", x: 480, y: 140, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Session", sub: "cookie, CSRF, idle timeout" },
  { id: "can", x: 250, y: 250, w: 420, h: 60, label: "can() and require()", sub: "one permission service", strong: true },
  { id: "roles", x: 710, y: 250, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Memberships", sub: "project and lab roles" },
  { id: "notify", x: 20, y: 370, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Notifications", sub: "readers at send time" },
  { id: "audit", x: 250, y: 370, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Audit log", sub: "person and agent" },
  { id: "webhooks", x: 480, y: 370, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Webhooks", sub: "identifiers, held()" },
  { id: "read", x: 710, y: 370, w: 190, h: 60, label: "Read scoping", sub: "readable projects, 404" },
];
const edges = [
  { from: "mcp", to: "token", label: "bearer" },
  { from: "oauth", to: "token", label: "live consent" },
  { from: "idp", to: "session", label: "signs in" },
  { from: "idp", to: "oauth" },
  { from: "token", to: "can" },
  { from: "session", to: "can" },
  { from: "can", to: "roles" },
  { from: "can", to: "read", label: "reads" },
  { from: "can", to: "audit", label: "changes" },
  { from: "audit", to: "webhooks" },
  { from: "audit", to: "notify" },
];

const H = {
  short: "Who may do what",
  how: "From a credential to an allowed or refused request",
  example: "An unattended QA agent acting for an analyst",
  rules: "Roles, tokens, delegation, reads and disclosure",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "read-seq", label: "A delegated read, step by step" },
  { id: "example", label: H.example },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Every request ends at one function, <code>can(user, action, target)</code>, which combines the
      caller's roles with what their credential allows. Project access comes only from membership: an
      Org Admin can see a project and manage its members, but reads its data only with a project role.
      A token carries scopes and may be held to some projects. An agent acting for a person is held
      to what both of them may do, down to the lab role that sets a state. Reads are filtered to the
      projects the caller can read now, and webhooks and notifications are checked against access
      again when they're sent.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Fixed org and project roles, decided in one place (<Adr n="0003" />).</li>
      <li>Project access comes only from membership (<Adr n="0024" />).</li>
      <li>Lab roles are declared by plugins and given per project (<Adr n="0116" />).</li>
      <li>A delegated action needs a lab role both identities hold (<Adr n="0210" />).</li>
    </ul>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      People sign in through the identity provider and use a session cookie. Clients, the CLI and
      agents use OAuth bearer tokens. Either way the request reaches the same permission service with
      a user and, for a token, its limits.
    </p>
    <Diagram id="perms" title="Credentials, the permission service, and what it guards" width={920} height={450} boxes={boxes} edges={edges}>
      <div slot="mcp">
        <p>
          MCP tools call the REST API in the same process, as the agent. The agent's identity travels
          in an ASGI scope key, not a header, so no client can forge it. Requests from a foreign <code
            >Origin</code
          > are refused, and tool answers are fenced with a random marker (<Adr n={[13, 49]} />).
        </p>
        <p>
          <Path p="server/src/iki_server/mcp/views.py" sym="def _same_origin(" />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/mcp/tools.py"
            sym="iki-data-"
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/security.py" sym='MCP_AGENT_KEY = "iki.mcp_agent"' />
        </p>
      </div>
      <div slot="oauth">
        <p>
          Iki is its own OAuth 2.1 server. A person consents to a client with scopes and, optionally,
          some projects. Unattended agents get a delegation that the client exchanges for the person's
          token (<Adr n={[28, 73]} />).
        </p>
        <p>
          <Path p={`${OAUTH}/scopes.py`} sym="SCOPES: dict" />, <Path p={`${OAUTH}/exchange.py`} sym="GRANT_TYPE" />, <Path
            p={`${OAUTH}/models.py`}
            sym="acts_for_people"
          />
        </p>
      </div>
      <div slot="idp">
        <p>
          Sign-in is single sign-on only, by OIDC or SAML; SCIM provisions people and teams. A first
          sign-in links to a provisioned account by external id, then user name, then a verified
          email (<Adr n={[23, 26, 151]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/accounts/linking.py" sym="MatchedBy" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/accounts/saml.py" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="token">
        <p>
          A bearer token's limits (its scopes, its projects, and the service account it acts
          alongside) are read from the consent as it stands now and attached to the user for the
          whole request. Narrowing a consent narrows tokens already issued (<Adr n="0152" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          <Path p={PERM} sym="class TokenLimits" />, <Path p={`${OAUTH}/access.py`} sym="def of(" />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/api/security.py"
            sym="permissions.limit_to(user, found.limits)"
          />
        </p>
      </div>
      <div slot="session">
        <p>
          The browser's API calls use the session cookie and need CSRF for unsafe methods. Requests the
          page makes on its own (<code>Iki-Background: 1</code>) don't extend the idle timeout (<Adr
            n={[27, 153]}
          />).
        </p>
        <p>
          <Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/security.py" sym="class SessionAuth" />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/accounts/session_policy.py"
            sym='BACKGROUND_HEADER = "Iki-Background"'
          />
        </p>
      </div>
      <div slot="can">
        <p>
          Views, the API, MCP and event streams all call <code>can</code> or <code>require</code>. It
          checks the action's org or project role, the token's scope for the action, the token's
          projects, and under a delegation the service account's roles too (<Adr n={[3, 24, 73]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={PERM} sym="def can(" />, <Path p={PERM} sym="ACTION_SCOPES" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/common.py" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="roles">
        <p>
          A membership is for one person or one team. A person's project role is the highest of their
          own and their teams'. Lab roles are granted on a membership and count only while an
          installed plugin declares them (<Adr n={[24, 116]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/org/models.py" sym="class LabRoleGrant" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/org/lab_roles.py" sym="def declared(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="read">
        <p>
          Projects the caller is a member of with a role, narrowed by the token; then entities linked
          into those projects. A miss is a 404 whether the thing is absent or unreadable (<Adr
            n={[24, 101]}
          />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p={PERM} sym="def visible_projects(" />, <Path p={ACCESS} sym="def readable_projects(" />, <Path p={ACCESS} sym="def visible_of" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="audit">
        <p>
          Every change is an append-only audit event naming the person and, when one acted, the agent:
          its client, grant, MCP session and the model it reported (<Adr n={[25, 72]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/audit/models.py" sym="class Agent(" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/audit/log.py" sym="agent=current_agent()" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="webhooks">
        <p>
          Events are built in the change's transaction and carry identifiers, never values. A webhook
          an app added sends only while the app's consent and roles would still let it add it, checked
          before every attempt (<Adr n={[31, 152]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/webhooks/events.py" sym="Identifiers only, never values" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/webhooks/services.py" sym="def held(" /></p>
      </div>
      <div slot="notify">
        <p>
          Recipients are filtered when the notification is made: only active people, not whoever made
          the change, and only those who can read the record. The inbox and the email task check again
          (<Adr n={[129, 159]} />).
        </p>
        <p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/notifications/declared.py" sym="access.readers(record, people)" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/notifications/services.py" sym="def inbox(" /></p>
      </div>
    </Diagram>

    <h3 id="read-seq">A delegated read, step by step</h3>
    <p>An unattended agent reads one sample, as the person who delegated to it.</p>
    <Sequence
      id="read-seq-dg"
      title="A delegated agent reads GET /api/v1/entities/SAM-000012"
      width={900}
      actors={[
        { id: "agent", label: "Agent", sub: "a service account's client" },
        { id: "oauth", label: "OAuth server" },
        { id: "api", label: "API", sub: "BearerAuth" },
        { id: "perm", label: "Permissions" },
        { id: "db", label: "Postgres" },
      ]}
      steps={[
        { id: "exchange", from: "agent", to: "oauth", label: "token exchange: the delegation's grant" },
        { id: "issued", from: "oauth", to: "agent", label: "the person's token, act: the client", dashed: true },
        { id: "get", from: "agent", to: "api", label: "GET /entities/SAM-000012, bearer" },
        { id: "limits", from: "api", to: "perm", label: "limits from the consent now; alongside: the bot" },
        { id: "filter", from: "perm", to: "db", label: "readable projects ∩ the token's ∩ the bot's" },
        { id: "exists", from: "db", to: "api", label: "EXISTS a link into one of them", dashed: true },
        { id: "answer", from: "api", to: "agent", label: "200 with the entity, or 404", dashed: true },
      ]}
    >
      <div slot="exchange"><p>RFC 8693 token exchange, with Iki's own <code>delegation</code> subject-token type. The client must be allowed to act for people, which is off by default; a delegation lasts at most 90 days and only people can give one (<Adr n="0073" />).</p><p><Path p={`${OAUTH}/exchange.py`} sym="DELEGATION =" />, <Path p={`${OAUTH}/services.py`} sym="MAX_DELEGATION_DAYS = 90" /></p></div>
      <div slot="issued"><p>The response names the client as the actor in <code>act</code>, so what the agent does is recorded as the person's, through the agent (<Adr n={[72, 73]} />).</p><p><Path p={`${OAUTH}/exchange.py`} sym='token["act"]' /></p></div>
      <div slot="get"><p>The API authenticates the bearer token on every request.</p><p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/security.py" /></p></div>
      <div slot="limits"><p>A delegated token's projects are the person's choice intersected with what the service account can see.</p><p><Path p={`${OAUTH}/access.py`} sym="projects & theirs" /></p></div>
      <div slot="filter"><p>Without the <code>read</code> scope the caller sees no projects at all. Org Admins skip the membership filter for seeing projects, but reading data still needs a role.</p><p><Path p={PERM} sym="def visible_projects(" />, <Path p={ACCESS} sym="my_role__isnull=False" /></p></div>
      <div slot="exists"><p>An entity is readable when it has a project link into a readable project (<Adr n="0035" />).</p><p><Path p={ACCESS} sym="def visible_of" /></p></div>
      <div slot="answer"><p>The lookup only ever searches what's readable, so an unreadable entity and a missing one answer the same 404 (<Adr n="0101" />).</p><p><Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/entities.py" sym="No such entity, or you can’t read it." /></p></div>
    </Sequence>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title={H.example}>
    <p>
      A lab runs a QA agent unattended. A scientist with the Editor role and the <code>analyst</code>
      lab role delegates to it, and the agent's service account is an Editor holding <code>qa</code>.
      The agent tries to move a sample to In testing, which analysts, reviewers and QA may all set.
      It's refused: each identity holds a role that may set the state, but not the same one. Once the
      service account is given <code>analyst</code> as well, the change goes through, and the revision
      names the scientist as the person and the agent's client and grant (<Adr n="0210" />).
    </p>
    <p>The lab roles, declared by the example LIMS plugin:</p>
    <Excerpt file="plugins/example_lims/plugin.yaml" start="^roles:" />
    <p>Who may set In testing:</p>
    <Excerpt file="plugins/example_lims/types/sample.yaml" start="^      in_testing:" />
    <p>
      Under a delegation, the lab roles that count are those both identities hold. The state change
      then needs one of the state's <code>set_by</code> roles among them:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file={PERM} start="^def lab_roles\(" />
    <p>
      The test that walks this through, refusal and success: <Path
        p="server/tests/test_delegated_lab_roles.py"
        sym="def test_delegated_state_changes_and_guards_use_the_same_shared_role("
      />. The same rule applies to guards, so a guard checking <code>"analyst" in actor.roles</code>
      sees the shared roles only (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/states.py" sym="if not permissions.lab_roles(user, owner) & set(roles):" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Org roles and project roles" open>
        <p>
          Org roles are Org Admin, Compute Admin and Auditor. Project roles are numbers, so "at least
          Editor" is a comparison, and each includes what the ones below it can do (<Adr n="0003" />):
        </p>
        <Excerpt file="server/src/iki_server/org/models.py" start="^class ProjectRole\(" />
        <p>The lowest project role each project action needs:</p>
        <Excerpt file={PERM} start="^_PROJECT_ACTION_ROLE = \{" />
        <p>
          Without a membership an Org Admin may only see a project and manage its members. Importing a
          bundle needs Org Admin as well as the project's Admin. An archived project allows only seeing,
          reading, managing, members and export (<Adr n={[24, 96]} />, <Path
            p={PERM}
            sym="_ORG_ADMIN_PROJECT_ACTIONS"
          />). A project the caller can't see answers 404; one they can see but can't act on, 403
          (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/common.py" sym="No such project, or you can’t see it." />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Lab roles">
        <p>
          Plugins declare lab roles; a project's Admin grants them on a membership, as <code
            >namespace/plugin.role</code
          >, checked by the database. A grant counts only while the holder has a project role and an
          installed plugin declares it. A state's <code>set_by</code> names the lab roles that may set
          it, and guards can test <code>actor.roles</code> (<Adr n={[116, 125, 128]} />, <Path
            p={PERM}
            sym="catalog.declared().keys()"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Service accounts and delegation">
        <p>
          A service account is a user of its own kind, with an address in a reserved <code>.invalid</code>
          domain, and can't be an Org Admin (<Adr n="0027" />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/accounts/models.py"
            sym="SERVICE_ACCOUNT_DOMAIN"
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/org/services.py" sym="Service accounts can’t be Org Admins." />).
        </p>
        <p>
          An unattended agent acts for a person by delegation: the person allows an Org Admin-approved
          client to act for them, for up to 90 days, and the client exchanges that for the person's
          token. Every check then runs against both: the person's roles and the service account's, the
          person's projects and the service account's, and the lab roles they share (<Adr
            n={[73, 210]}
          />). No token can approve a run, whatever its scopes, so an agent can't approve its own
          request (<Adr n="0050" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/approvals.py" sym="Tokens can't, whatever their scopes" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          A scheduled trigger runs as a chosen service account, on the authority of the Admin who
          turned it on (<Adr n="0081" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/models.py" sym="run_as = models.ForeignKey(" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Scopes and project-limited tokens">
        <p>Each action maps to one scope; the scopes a consent can grant:</p>
        <Excerpt file={`${OAUTH}/scopes.py`} start="^SCOPES: dict" />
        <p>
          A token given some projects acts only in them. A refusal that comes only from that choice
          answers 403 with the code <code>token_limited_to_projects</code>, so a client can tell it
          apart from a missing role (<Adr n="0152" />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/api/common.py"
            sym="token_limited_to_projects"
          />). Event streams check the credential again before each event (<Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/realtime/views.py"
            sym="Check = Callable[[], bool]"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="How a read is scoped">
        <p>
          <code>visible_projects</code> keeps projects with a membership for the caller, or all of them
          for an Org Admin; none without the <code>read</code> scope; and only the token's projects.
          <code>readable_projects</code> then drops those where the caller has no role, which is why an
          Org Admin sees a project but not its data. An entity is readable when it's linked into a
          readable project. Every kind of sharing (linking, references, run inputs, reuse) goes
          through one more check, <code>shareable_into</code> (<Adr n={[24, 67, 115]} />, <Path
            p={ACCESS}
            sym="def shareable_into("
          />).
        </p>
        <p>
          The access matrix lists every operation, and the IDOR tests replay each with another
          project's objects (<Adr n="0101" />, <Path p="server/tests/test_api_access.py" />, <Path
            p="server/tests/test_api_idor.py"
          />, <Path p="server/tests/test_worker_idor.py" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="An agent's changes name the person and the agent">
        <p>
          Each agent is stored once, append-only: client id and name, grant, MCP session, and the
          software and model it reported. Every audit event and revision points to it. The model comes
          from a header Iki can't verify, <code>Iki-Agent-Model</code>, and is shown as reported (<Adr
            n="0072"
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/security.py" sym='MODEL_HEADER = "Iki-Agent-Model"' />).
        </p>
        <Excerpt file="server/src/iki_server/audit/models.py" start="^class Via\(" />
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="What webhooks and notifications disclose">
        <p>
          A webhook's entity payload carries identifiers only, never field values, plus the actor and
          the agent; the receiver reads values through the API with its own access (<Adr
            n={[31, 72]}
          />). Sending is re-checked before every attempt, including retries (<Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/webhooks/tasks.py"
            sym="if not services.may_send(webhook):"
          />).
        </p>
        <p>
          A notification names the record whose change it tells of. It goes only to active people who
          can read that record and any record it refers to, never to service accounts or imported
          people, and not to whoever made the change. The inbox is filtered again when read, and the
          email task checks once more before sending (<Adr n={[129, 159]} />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/notifications/tasks.py"
            sym="no longer theirs to see"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Database roles and the audit log">
        <p>
          The app connects as a role that can't change the schema; only the migrator owns tables. A
          web pod whose role is too powerful never turns ready, and granting privileges refuses to run
          when the two roles are the same (<Adr n="0025" />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/core/db_roles.py"
            sym="rolsuper"
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/core/management/commands/grant_app_privileges.py" sym="The app and the migrator are both" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Plugins' frames">
        <p>
          Plugin components run in sandboxed frames with <code>connect-src 'none'</code>, pinned by
          digest, and a script run first removes what no policy covers, such as WebRTC and nested
          frames. Exfiltration is tested in three browsers (<Adr n={[84, 102]} />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/web/frame_harden.js"
          />). The <a href="/web-app/#frame-seq">web app</a> follows a frame from load to message.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0025" /> lists the audit channels as web, API, MCP, command line or system; the
            code adds <code>scim</code> and <code>trigger</code>, from <Adr n={[26, 81]} />.
          </li>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0003" /> says Editors run on "backend and client tiers"; the code's actions are
            <code>run.self_hosted</code> and <code>run.backend</code> for Editors.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={PERM} />: actions, roles, scopes and <code>can</code></li>
        <li><Path p={ACCESS} />: what a caller can read and share</li>
        <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/oauth" />: the OAuth server, consent and delegation</li>
        <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/accounts" />: sign-in, SCIM, sessions, service accounts</li>
        <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/audit" />: the audit log and agents</li>
        <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/mcp" />: the MCP endpoint and tools</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0003" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0024" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0027" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0028" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0049" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0072" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0073" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0116" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0152" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0210" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
